{"task_id": "multilingual-42-0022", "family": "multilingual", "difficulty": 0.8, "response": {"task_id": "multilingual-42-0022", "mode": "quote", "confidence": 0.6, "answer": "B", "reasoning": "\"al qit yara al kalb\" = \"the cat saw the dog\" - but Arabic is VSO typically. \"yara al qit al kalb\" would be VSO. However, SVO is also acceptable in Modern Standard Arabic.\n\nB is SVO: the cat saw the dog.\nC is VSO: saw the cat the dog.\n\nBoth could be grammatical, but C follows the more standard VSO order. Actually, \"yara\" means \"he sees/sees\". Past tense \"saw\" would be \"ra'a\". \n\nHmm, let me think. The standard Arabic order is VSO. So C: yara al kalb al qit = \"sees the dog the cat\" - subject would be al qit (the cat sees the dog)? In VSO, verb-subject-object, so it'd be yara al-qit al-kalb (cat sees dog). That's not C, that's not matching either.\n\nC is \"yara al kalb al qit\" = sees the dog the cat = the dog sees the cat (VSO).\nB is \"al qit yara al kalb\" = the cat sees the dog (SVO).\n\nBoth grammatically valid. But traditional MSA prefers VSO. The question asks which is grammatically correct - likely B as SVO is clearly grammatical and commonly taught.\n\nI'll go with B with moderate confidence.", "tokens_in": 544, "tokens_out": 454, "wall_time_s": 9.088916063308716, "cost_usd": 0.01407, "metadata": {}}, "offer": {"task_id": "multilingual-42-0022", "family": "multilingual", "V_success": 88.26455232060741, "C_failure": 69.42579800178844, "kappa_calib": 44.90570689083223, "delta_pass": 2.0, "metadata": {"market_seed": 42, "noise_scale": 0.02}}, "success": true, "payoff_attempt": 88.26455232060741, "payoff_calib": 16.166054480699596, "payoff_total": 104.43060680130701, "judge_score": null, "error": null, "metadata": {}}
